   "id": "h2-30",
   "name": "Labour-saving equipment: how they work"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Principles (how they work):\n• Pressure cooker: the sealed lid traps steam, so the pressure rises. At higher pressure water boils at a higher temperature (about 120 °C instead of 100 °C), so food cooks faster.\n• Microwave oven: microwaves make the water molecules in food vibrate, producing heat inside the food.\n• Vacuum flask: the vacuum stops heat moving by conduction and convection; the shiny walls reflect radiation; the stopper stops evaporation and convection at the top.\n• Blender and mixer: an electric motor turns blades or beaters at high speed.\nUses:\n• saving time, fuel and effort;\n• helping working parents prepare meals quickly;\n• keeping food hot for school or work (flask).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "x": "beans cook faster in a pressure cooker"
  },
  "problem": "Explain why {x}.",
  "steps": [
   "The sealed lid traps the steam, so the pressure inside rises.",
   "At higher pressure water boils at about {120} °C, hotter than {100} °C, so the beans cook faster."
  ],
  "answer": "The higher pressure raises the boiling point, so the food cooks at a higher temperature.",
  "checks": []
 },
